_refine_special_details          
;
Refinement of F^2^ against ALL reflections. The weighted R-factor wR and
goodness of fit S are based on F^2^, conventional R-factors R are based
on F, with F set to zero for negative F^2^. The threshold expression of
F^2^ > 2sigma(F^2^) is used only for calculating R-factors(gt) etc. and is
not relevant to the choice of reflections for refinement. R-factors based
on F^2^ are statistically about twice as large as those based on F, and R-
factors based on ALL data will be even larger.

Six most disagreeable reflections were omitted in the refinement.

C4A and C4B were the two components of a disordered carbon atom.
The sof of C4A was refined as a free variable to converge at 0.53.
C3A and C3B were two independent carbon atoms in an imidazole ring.
This portion of the ring was disordered over two sites related by a two-fold
axis. Therefore, the sofs of both C3A and C3B were fixed at 0.50 and
a PART command was applied to exclude spurious bonds between the
disordered parts. All these disordered carbon atoms were only refined
isotropically. However, hydrogen atoms connected to these carbon atoms
were included by using AFIX commands.

A large residue electron density peak (about 2 -e/A^-3^) inside the pore at
Wyckoff position 8e (2mm.) was assigned as O1, and this oxygen atom was
refined anisotropically. By using this model, the largest electron density peak
was reduced to less than 1.0 -e/A^-3^. To further treat the diffuse electron
density, SWAT command was applied. The two variables g and U were refined
to converge at 1.1 and 2.9, respectively.
;

